_type_from_rhs has two branches that name a type from the right-hand side of a
declaration. The composite_literal branch was fixed to preserve the package
qualifier -- &http.Client{} -> http.Client -- because a bare Client resolves to
no package and lands in the "external" module slot where the catalogue cannot
match it. Its neighbour, the NewXxx() constructor-naming branch, still dropped
it: bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin) bound a bare "Reader", and reader.ReadString('\n')
emitted go:external:0-0:ReadString:unresolved.
_type_identifier_from_node's own docstring states the contract the branch
violated: the full qualified name "is critical for IO boundary detection".

Measured over four receiver spellings of one declaration, the two nobody writes
resolved and the two everybody writes did not:

  reader := bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin)             -> "Reader"        dead
  var reader *bufio.Reader = bufio.NewReader(..)  -> "Reader"        dead
  var reader *bufio.Reader; reader = ...          -> "bufio.Reader"  live
  func f(reader *bufio.Reader)                    -> "bufio.Reader"  live

The declared-type case is the sharp one: the qualified type is written literally
in the source and the worse inference still won, because the "prefer the concrete
type over the declared (interface) type" rule cannot tell a concrete type from a
guess off a function's name. a.b.NewFoo() now abstains rather than inventing an
"a.b" package -- in expression position that operand is a value and the call is a
method, so a qualified name import_aliases cannot resolve is worse than none.

THIS IS THE BLOCKER WI-vutav NAMED WRONGLY. The item reads "the transfer calls
are absent from the catalogue" and adding them was to be the work. The fixtures
